## 🔴 Step 1 – Naive / Force Approach (Ugly but Easy)
Think of it like merging two arrays manually.

👉 Idea:
- Create a **new linked list**.  
- Each time, compare `l1` and `l2`.  
- Allocate a new node with the smaller value, append it.  
- Continue until one list is exhausted, then copy the rest.  

### Code (Force Approach)
```c
#include <stdlib.h>

struct ListNode* mergeTwoLists(struct ListNode* l1, struct ListNode* l2) {
    if (l1 == NULL) return l2;
    if (l2 == NULL) return l1;

    struct ListNode* head = NULL;
    struct ListNode* tail = NULL;

    while (l1 != NULL && l2 != NULL) {
        struct ListNode* newNode = (struct ListNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct ListNode));
        if (l1->val < l2->val) {
            newNode->val = l1->val;
            l1 = l1->next;
        } else {
            newNode->val = l2->val;
            l2 = l2->next;
        }
        newNode->next = NULL;

        if (head == NULL) {   // first node
            head = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        } else {
            tail->next = newNode;
            tail = newNode;
        }
    }

    // Copy remaining nodes
    while (l1 != NULL) {
        struct ListNode* newNode = (struct ListNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct ListNode));
        newNode->val = l1->val;
        newNode->next = NULL;
        tail->next = newNode;
        tail = newNode;
        l1 = l1->next;
    }

    while (l2 != NULL) {
        struct ListNode* newNode = (struct ListNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct ListNode));
        newNode->val = l2->val;
        newNode->next = NULL;
        tail->next = newNode;
        tail = newNode;
        l2 = l2->next;
    }

    return head;
}
```

👉 This works, but it’s **wasteful**:
- Allocates **new nodes** instead of reusing existing ones.  
- Takes more memory, more code, harder to maintain.  

